    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <math.h>\\n\\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\\n\\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\\n\\nvoid body_force(float *px, float *py, float *pz,\\n                float *vx, float *vy, float *vz,\\n                int n, float dt) {\\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n    float fx = 0.0f;\\n    float fy = 0.0f;\\n    float fz = 0.0f;\\n\\n    float xi = px[i];\\n    float yi = py[i];\\n    float zi = pz[i];\\n\\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n      float dx = px[j] - xi;\\n      float dy = py[j] - yi;\\n      float dz = pz[j] - zi;\\n      float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\\n      float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\\n      float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\\n\\n      fx += dx * inv_dist3;\\n      fy += dy * inv_dist3;\\n      fz += dz * inv_dist3;\\n    }\\n\\n    vx[i] += dt*fx;\\n    vy[i] += dt*fy;\\n    vz[i] += dt*fz;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError reading nbodie value\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError reading input values\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n\\n  return bodies;\\n}\\n\\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\\\"\\\\nError writing to output\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\\n  int nbodies;\\n\\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\\n\\n  float *px = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n  float *py = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n  float *pz = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n  float *vx = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n  float *vy = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n  float *vz = (float *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\\n\\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n    px[i] = bodies[i].x;\\n    py[i] = bodies[i].y;\\n    pz[i] = bodies[i].z;\\n    vx[i] = bodies[i].vx;\\n    vy[i] = bodies[i].vy;\\n    vz[i] = bodies[i].vz;\\n  }\\n\\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\\n    body_force(px, py, pz, vx, vy, vz, nbodies, DT);\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n      px[i] += vx[i] * DT;\\n      py[i] += vy[i] * DT;\\n      pz[i] += vz[i] * DT;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\\n    bodies[i].x = px[i];\\n    bodies[i].y = py[i];\\n    bodies[i].z = pz[i];\\n    bodies[i].vx = vx[i];\\n    bodies[i].vy = vy[i];\\n    bodies[i].vz = vz[i];\\n  }\\n\\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\\n\\n  free(px); free(py); free(pz);\\n  free(vx); free(vy); free(vz);\\n  free(bodies);\\n\\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/nbody.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Converting the AoS body array into separate SoA float arrays improves cache line utilization when streaming through all bodies per force evaluation, while OpenMP static scheduling across the outer i-loop provides near-linear speedup on 4 cores without altering floating-point summation order.\",\n  \"notes\": \"The transformation preserves exact per-body j-summation order: each body's forces are accumulated sequentially over j=0..n-1 in both the original and modified code. The SoA layout allows contiguous access to x, y, z streams, improving spatial locality without changing arithmetic operations or rounding.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Structure-of-arrays (SoA) data layout for positions and velocities\",\n    \"OpenMP parallelization over the outer body-index loop with static scheduling\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. We are role A: memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. Current source is baseline nbody.c (sequential). The task: modify only src/nbody.c. Need to produce a candidate that improves performance while preserving correctness exactly.\n\nObservations from history:\n- Baseline mean 0.671s.\n- Several experiments achieved ~0.222s with SoA + OpenMP? Actually some got 0.161s, e.g., experiment 10af5e5f3ddc484d score 0.806 (mean 0.1616), and 704fffe5d2a849b5 mean 0.1772, and combination with e430f80588d44b85 got 0.1555s.\n- The best observed is 0.155495 from sources [\"704fffe5d2a849b5\", \"e430f80588d44b85\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] mean 0.1555, score 0.81194.\n- Our role A experiments so far have been around 0.222s (SoA + blocking but maybe not as good). The best ones likely used OpenMP parallelization and SoA layout.\n\nWe need to propose a new experiment that improves further. We can use ideas from previous successful sources? But integration_source_ids is empty, so we cannot keep any source IDs; we must write our own code based on understanding. However we can incorporate techniques: structure-of-arrays (SoA), cache blocking over i-loop, OpenMP parallelization over blocks/tiles, maybe vectorization hints.\n\nThe current baseline uses AoS struct Body with 6 floats. The force computation is O(N^2) per iteration, and there are 10 iterations. N likely large enough to matter. We need exact output bytes: the reference computes in same order? Actually correctness compares against official reference (sequential implementation). Since floating-point operations may differ if we change summation order or use different arithmetic; but the problem says \"retaining the correctness of the solution\" and comparison is exact output bytes against official reference. The baseline sequential code likely matches reference exactly because it's the provided serial code? Actually the judge compares our output to the original sequential implementation (the one given). So we must produce bitwise identical results as the baseline sequential code, unless the problem allows tolerance? It says \"Exact output bytes against official reference\". So any change that alters floating-point rounding may fail. Therefore we need to preserve exact same operations and order for each body's force accumulation.\n\nThe baseline computes for each i: fx=fy=fz=0; then loops j from 0..n-1, computing dx = p[j].x - p[i].x etc., sqrd_dist = dx*dx+dy*dy+dz*dz + SOFTENING (float), inv_dist = 1/sqrt(sqrd_dist) (float sqrt? Actually sqrt returns double but assigned to float; in C, sqrt is double, so conversion). Then inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist (float multiplications). fx += dx*inv_dist3 etc. This order must be preserved exactly for each i and j.\n\nIf we parallelize over i with OpenMP, the per-i computation remains identical; only different threads compute different i's independently. That should preserve exact results because no cross-body dependency in force accumulation (each body's forces independent). The integration step also independent per body. So OpenMP parallelization is safe as long as each thread computes its assigned i's exactly same order.\n\nSo we can use OpenMP to parallelize the outer loop over i, with static scheduling perhaps. But need to ensure no race conditions: p[i] updated after force computation; but different threads write different p[i], so fine. However if we block and preload positions into local arrays, that may change nothing as long as values are same.\n\nThe best observed 0.1555 likely used OpenMP + SoA? Let's infer from source IDs: \"704fffe5d2a849b5\" maybe a SoA transformation; \"e430f80588d44b85\" maybe OpenMP parallelization; \"9adc054f311d4298\" maybe something else. But we don't have those sources.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate relevant to role A: memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. We can combine SoA layout with cache blocking over i-loop and OpenMP parallelization over blocks/tiles. However previous role A attempts with SoA+blocking got ~0.222s, not as good as 0.1555. Maybe the best used a different approach: perhaps using OpenMP on j loop? But that would change order if reduction is done carefully; but could preserve per-i accumulation by having each thread accumulate partial sums for all i? That's more complex.\n\nLet's think about performance characteristics. Baseline sequential O(N^2) with AoS, poor cache locality because p[j] accessed sequentially (good), p[i] reused across j (but only one body). Actually inner loop over j reads p[j] sequentially and p[i] constant; that's good for memory: each iteration reads 6 floats from consecutive bodies. So AoS is actually fine for sequential access pattern. The main cost is O(N^2) floating point operations, not memory? For large N, memory bandwidth may matter but inner loop streams through all bodies once per i, so total data read ~N^2 * sizeof(Body). With cache blocking over i (tiles), we can reuse p[j] across multiple i's within a tile, reducing main-memory traffic. That should help.\n\nBut previous role A attempts with SoA+blocking got 0.222s vs baseline 0.671s, speedup ~3x. The best observed 0.1555 is ~4.3x speedup. Maybe they used OpenMP across 4 cores? Baseline sequential uses one core; if we parallelize over i with OpenMP on 4 threads, ideal speedup up to 4x (if memory bandwidth not limiting). So 0.671/4 = 0.168s, close to best observed 0.1555. So likely the best used OpenMP across 4 cores without much blocking? But role A experiments with OpenMP+blocking got ~0.222s, which is slower than pure OpenMP maybe due to overhead or poor scheduling.\n\nLet's examine history: experiment f2a4207ec8704f6d (role A) hypothesis \"Cache-blocking over the body index i (block size 4) combined with a structure-of-arrays layout ... OpenMP parallelizes over blocks\" got mean 0.2225s. That is slower than best 0.1555. So blocking may hurt due to overhead or because memory traffic already okay? Or maybe block size too small.\n\nExperiment 545a1fb8e9284bf4: \"Cache-blocking the i-loop into tiles of 64 bodies, preloading each tile's positions into local arrays, and parallelizing over tiles ... SIMD hint\" got 0.2233s. Also slower.\n\nSo maybe pure OpenMP without blocking is better? But we don't have a direct observation of that from role A; but the best observed (from other roles?) likely used OpenMP with SoA or something.\n\nWe need to propose one experiment. We can try: use OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static scheduling, and maybe keep AoS layout (since inner loop already sequential). But we must ensure exact same floating-point operations per body. Parallelizing outer loop is safe. That could give near 4x speedup on 4 cores if memory bandwidth sufficient. Baseline 0.671s -> ~0.168s expected. The best observed 0.1555 suggests maybe also some vectorization or better cache.\n\nBut we are role A, so we should focus on memory locality/blocking/data layout. We can combine OpenMP with a simple blocking over i to improve cache reuse of p[j] across multiple i's. However previous attempts didn't beat pure parallel? Maybe because block size too small or overhead. Let's try a larger tile size and use SoA? But SoA may not help much since inner loop already sequential; but it could allow vectorization if compiler auto-vectorizes the j-loop over bodies? Actually with AoS, each body has 6 floats contiguous; accessing p[j].x etc. are strided by sizeof(Body)=24 bytes, so vectorizing across j is hard because x values not contiguous. SoA would make arrays of x,y,z,vx,vy,vz separate, allowing SIMD loads for dx = px[j]-px[i] etc. That could improve performance significantly if compiler auto-vectorizes the inner loop over j. But we must preserve exact order: vectorization with reductions might change summation order? If compiler uses SIMD to compute multiple j's simultaneously and then reduces, that changes floating-point addition order, causing different rounding. So we cannot allow auto-vectorization of the reduction unless we use explicit scalar accumulation in same order. However we could manually unroll but still sequential additions.\n\nBut maybe the best observed used OpenMP only without vectorization? Let's estimate: 0.1555s on 4 cores vs baseline 0.671s gives speedup ~4.32, which is above ideal 4x due to cache effects (blocking) or compiler optimizations like -O3 with auto-vectorization maybe.\n\nWe need to propose a candidate that likely improves over previous role A attempts and possibly beats best observed. Since we don't have access to other sources, we can design our own.\n\nLet's consider using OpenMP parallel for over i with schedule(static) and perhaps #pragma omp simd on inner loop? But SIMD would change order unless we use reduction with fixed order? Actually if compiler vectorizes the j-loop, it will compute partial sums in vector lanes and then combine; that changes rounding. So not safe.\n\nAlternative: Use OpenMP parallelization over i but also block j to improve cache reuse across multiple i's within a thread? But each thread handles a contiguous chunk of i's (static schedule). Within that chunk, for each i it reads all p[j] sequentially. If the chunk is large enough, p[j] array may not fit in L2/L3; but if we block over j as well, we can reuse p[j] across multiple i's within a tile. However previous attempts did that and got slower.\n\nMaybe the best approach is simply OpenMP parallel for with no blocking, because memory bandwidth on 4 cores might be enough to stream all data once per i? Actually each thread reads entire p array for each of its assigned i's; total reads = N * (N/threads) * sizeof(Body). With 4 threads, that's same as sequential total reads? No: sequential does N^2 reads. Parallel with static schedule over i: each thread processes ~N/4 i's, and for each i it reads all N bodies, so total reads = N * (N/4) per thread, sum across threads = N^2, same as sequential. So no reduction in memory traffic; but parallel execution uses multiple cores to compute concurrently, limited by memory bandwidth if all threads stream simultaneously. If memory bandwidth is saturated, speedup may be less than 4x.\n\nBlocking over i (tiles) reduces total reads: for a tile of T bodies, we read p[j] once per tile? Actually algorithm: for each tile of i's, loop j from 0..N-1 and compute forces for all i in tile using same p[j]. That way p[j] is loaded once per tile, reused across T i's. Total reads = (N/T) * N * sizeof(Body) = N^2/T. So blocking reduces memory traffic by factor T. This should help if memory-bound. But previous attempts with T=4 or 64 got ~0.222s, which is slower than pure parallel? Wait baseline sequential 0.671; pure OpenMP maybe not measured but best observed 0.1555 likely includes some optimization. Our role A blocking+SoA got 0.22, so it's faster than baseline (3x) but not as fast as best.\n\nMaybe the issue is that with SoA and blocking, we preload tile positions into local arrays, which adds overhead; or block size too small causing loop overhead. Let's try a larger tile size like N/4? But then each thread handles one tile of ~N/4 i's, and inside it loops over all j once per tile, so memory traffic reduced by factor 4 (since 4 threads). That could give good speedup if memory-bound.\n\nBut we must preserve exact order: for each i in tile, the inner loop over j must be exactly same as sequential. If we restructure to outer loop over j and inner over i within tile, that changes summation order per body? Actually force accumulation for a given i sums over all j; if we swap loops (j outer, i inner), then for each i we would accumulate contributions from j in increasing j order only if we process all j before moving to next i. But if we do nested: for tile of i's, for j=0..N-1, for each i in tile compute contribution and add to fx[i], fy[i], fz[i]. That still accumulates per i over j in same order (j increasing) because inner loop over i doesn't affect the sequence of additions for a given i: we process j=0, then for all i's add dx*inv_dist3; then j=1, etc. For each i, the additions happen in j order 0..N-1, same as original. So it preserves exact floating-point operations per body (assuming inv_dist computed identically). However note that in original code, for a given i, fx is accumulated sequentially: fx += dx*inv_dist3; fy += ...; fz += ...; The compiler may use FMA? But with -O3 maybe. If we restructure to j outer and i inner, the order of additions per i remains same (j increasing), but the actual floating-point operations are identical because each addition is independent for different i's. So it should produce bitwise identical results.\n\nThus we can implement a blocked algorithm: partition i into tiles; for each tile, initialize fx/fy/fz arrays for that tile to zero; then loop over j from 0..N-1, and inside loop over i in tile compute dx etc., inv_dist3 (same for all i? No, depends on p[i] so different per i), but we can load p[j] once and reuse across i's. This reduces memory reads of p[j] by factor TILE_SIZE.\n\nWe also need to parallelize over tiles with OpenMP. Each thread handles one or more tiles independently; no shared data except reading p array (read-only) and writing velocities for bodies in tile after force accumulation. Since each body belongs to exactly one tile, writes are independent across threads. So safe.\n\nThis is a classic cache-blocking transformation. We can choose TILE_SIZE such that the tile's positions fit in L1/L2? But we also need arrays fx,fy,fz per tile (3 floats * TILE). If TILE=64, that's small. Previous attempts used 4 or 64 and got ~0.22s. Maybe because they preloaded p[j] into local arrays? Actually the inner loop over j still reads p[j] from main memory each time for each tile; but with blocking, p[j] is read once per tile instead of once per i. So total reads reduced by TILE. For N large (maybe 100k?), this should help.\n\nWhy did previous attempts not beat pure OpenMP? Perhaps because they used SoA layout which requires converting input to separate arrays, adding overhead; or because the compiler already optimized sequential code well enough that memory traffic wasn't bottleneck? Let's estimate: baseline 0.671s for N maybe 10000? O(N^2) operations ~1e8 per iteration *10 = 1e9 ops. At 4 cores with OpenMP, if compute-bound, could get ~0.17s. The best observed 0.1555 suggests near ideal.\n\nMaybe the previous role A attempts had overhead from preloading tile positions into local arrays (copying) and using SoA which may not be beneficial because inner loop already sequential; also they used SIMD hint but that might have changed order? They were correct though, so maybe compiler didn't vectorize due to dependency or it did with reduction in same order? Hard to know.\n\nWe need a new idea. Since we are role A (memory locality), perhaps try using OpenMP parallelization over i-loop without blocking but with #pragma omp simd on inner loop? But that may change rounding; however if compiler uses SIMD with horizontal sum, it changes order. Not safe unless we use -ffast-math? No.\n\nMaybe we can manually unroll the j-loop by 4 and accumulate in same order? Unrolling preserves order if we do sequential additions: fx += term0; fx += term1; etc. That could improve ILP but not memory traffic. But compiler already does that with -O3.\n\nAnother idea: use OpenMP tasks or dynamic scheduling to balance load, but static is fine for uniform N.\n\nGiven the best observed 0.1555 likely used OpenMP + maybe SoA? Let's look at source IDs from interactions: The interaction with sources [\"704fffe5d2a849b5\", \"e430f80588d44b85\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] got mean 0.1555. We don't know what those are, but they might be from other roles (B,C,D). However we can infer that combining three ideas gave best.\n\nSince integration_source_ids is empty, we cannot reuse them; we must write our own code. But we can try to replicate likely optimizations: SoA layout + OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule, and maybe cache blocking? Let's design a candidate:\n\n- Convert Body array into separate arrays px, py, pz, vx, vy, vz (SoA) for better vectorization potential.\n- Use OpenMP parallel for over i with schedule(static).\n- Inside each i, compute forces using scalar code exactly as original but reading from SoA arrays. This preserves order per i because j loop sequential and additions in same order.\n\nBut will this be faster than AoS? With SoA, the inner loop reads px[j], py[j], pz[j] which are contiguous across j (stride 4 bytes), so compiler can vectorize loads but not reductions safely. However scalar code may still benefit from cache line utilization: each cache line holds many x values, whereas in AoS each body is 24 bytes and a cache line holds ~2-3 bodies; SoA allows better spatial locality for each coordinate stream. But the original inner loop reads all six fields per j; with SoA it would read three separate arrays (x,y,z) sequentially, which may be more cache-friendly because each array is contiguous. However we also need velocities? Not in force computation.\n\nBut if we use OpenMP parallelization over i, each thread streams through px,py,pz arrays for its assigned i's; that could improve memory bandwidth utilization due to sequential access patterns across threads (each thread reads same arrays but at different offsets? Actually all threads read the entire arrays for each of their i's, so they compete for memory bandwidth). SoA might help because each array is contiguous and can be prefetched.\n\nLet's try a simple OpenMP parallelization with AoS first? But we need to propose one candidate; we can choose SoA + OpenMP. However previous role A attempts already did SoA+blocking, not pure SoA+OpenMP without blocking. Maybe that will be better.\n\nAlternatively, use OpenMP parallel for over i and also add #pragma omp simd on inner loop with reduction? But to preserve order, we can't vectorize the accumulation across j because it's a sequential dependency (fx depends on previous fx). However we could use SIMD to compute multiple dx*inv_dist3 terms in parallel and then combine using ordered addition? Not straightforward.\n\nMaybe we can use OpenMP 4.0 simd with reduction(+:fx) but that would change order; not safe.\n\nSo likely the best approach is just OpenMP parallelization over i, possibly with SoA for cache efficiency. Let's test mentally: baseline sequential AoS 0.671s. If we add OpenMP parallel for over i (with default static), on 4 cores we might get ~0.17-0.20s if memory bandwidth not limiting. The best observed 0.1555 suggests maybe also some cache blocking or SoA.\n\nWe can propose a candidate that combines:\n1. Structure-of-arrays layout: separate arrays for x,y,z,vx,vy,vz.\n2. OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule.\n3. Cache blocking over j? Actually we could block the inner loop over j into chunks to improve cache reuse across multiple i's within a thread? But if each thread handles many i's sequentially, it will read all p[j] for each i; no reuse unless we restructure.\n\nMaybe use OpenMP parallelization over tiles of i (blocking) with tile size chosen as N/num_threads so each thread gets one contiguous block of i's. Within that block, for each i it reads all j sequentially; but across different i's in same block, p[j] is re-read from memory each time because no reuse unless we also block over j and keep a tile of j's in cache? Actually if the block of i's is large (N/4), then for each i we stream through entire p array. That doesn't reduce memory traffic compared to pure parallel; it just partitions work.\n\nTo reduce memory traffic, we need to reuse p[j] across multiple i's within a tile. So we should use two-level blocking: outer over tiles of i (size TILE_I), inner loop over j from 0..N-1, and inside that compute forces for all i in the tile using current p[j]. This way each p[j] is read once per tile instead of once per i. Then parallelize over tiles.\n\nLet's implement this with SoA or AoS? We can keep AoS to avoid conversion overhead; but reading p[j] as struct gives 6 floats contiguous, which is fine for one j at a time. The inner loop over i in tile will read p[i] (tile bodies) repeatedly across j, but those are small and fit in cache if TILE_I small enough.\n\nWe need to preserve exact order per body: For each i in tile, we accumulate fx_i += dx*inv_dist3 for j=0..N-1. If we loop j outer and i inner, the additions for a given i happen in increasing j order because we process all i's for j=0, then j=1, etc. So yes.\n\nImplementation sketch:\n\nvoid body_force(Body *p, float dt, int n) {\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int tile = 0; tile < num_tiles; ++tile) {\n    int i_start = tile * TILE;\n    int i_end = min(i_start + TILE, n);\n    // local force accumulators\n    float fx[TILE], fy[TILE], fz[TILE];\n    for (int i = i_start; i < i_end; ++i) { fx[i-i_start]=0; ... }\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      // load p[j] once\n      float px_j = p[j].x, py_j = p[j].y, pz_j = p[j].z;\n      for (int i = i_start; i < i_end; ++i) {\n        int idx = i - i_start;\n        float dx = px_j - p[i].x;\n        ...\n        // compute inv_dist3 same as original\n        fx[idx] += dx * inv_dist3;\n        ...\n      }\n    }\n    for (int i = i_start; i < i_end; ++i) {\n      int idx = i-i_start;\n      p[i].vx += dt*fx[idx]; ...\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nBut note: in original, the force computation uses p[j] and p[i] values at start of iteration. Since we don't modify positions during body_force (only velocities), it's fine.\n\nWe must ensure that inv_dist3 is computed exactly same as original: sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING; inv_dist = 1/sqrt(sqrd_dist); inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist. All float operations in same order.\n\nAlso note that in original, the inner loop uses p[j].x etc., and p[i] is constant for each i. In our restructured version, we compute dx = px_j - p[i].x; same expression. The multiplication order: fx += dx * inv_dist3; same. So bitwise identical.\n\nNow choose TILE size. We want to balance memory reuse vs overhead. If TILE too large, the local arrays fx/fy/fz may not fit in registers/L1 and cause spills; also the inner loop over i within tile will be repeated for each j, so if TILE is large (e.g., 256), we have many floating-point operations per j but that's fine. However the p[i] values are read repeatedly across j; they should stay in cache if TILE small enough to fit all positions of tile bodies in L1/L2. Each body has 6 floats = 24 bytes. For TILE=64, that's 1536 bytes for positions (x,y,z) plus velocities? Actually we only need x,y,z for force; but p[i] struct includes vx etc., so reading whole struct may bring extra data. We could use SoA to read only x,y,z arrays.\n\nBut let's first try AoS with TILE maybe 64 or 128. Previous attempts used 4 and 64, got ~0.22s. Maybe because they also had OpenMP overhead? Let's see: if we parallelize over tiles, each thread handles multiple tiles; but the inner loop over j is sequential across all j for a tile. That means each thread processes its assigned tiles one by one, and within each tile it loops over all N bodies (j). So total work per thread = (#tiles_per_thread) * N * TILE operations. With static schedule, if num_tiles = n/TILE, and threads=4, each gets ~num_tiles/4 tiles. Total reads of p[j] across all threads: each tile reads all N bodies once, so total reads = num_tiles * N = (n/TILE)*N = n^2/TILE. That's reduced by TILE compared to sequential n^2. Good.\n\nBut the inner loop over i within tile adds overhead: for each j, we compute forces for TILE i's. This is essentially same number of floating-point ops as original (TILE per j), but now we have nested loops with index calculations. The compiler may unroll or vectorize? Possibly not due to dependencies on fx array.\n\nWhy did previous attempts get 0.22s instead of better? Maybe because TILE=4 too small, overhead dominates; TILE=64 maybe still not enough reuse? Or because they used SoA and preloaded tile positions into local arrays (copying) which adds cost. Let's try a larger TILE like 256 or 512 to increase reuse factor, but then the inner loop over i may cause register pressure if we keep fx/fy/fz in registers; compiler might spill to stack. We can use arrays on stack with fixed size? But variable length arrays not standard C99? Actually VLA allowed in C99 but maybe not safe for large TILE. We could allocate dynamically per thread, but that adds overhead.\n\nAlternatively, we can restructure without local force arrays: For each tile, we could loop over i within tile and then j inside, but that would read p[j] multiple times (once per i), losing reuse. So need j outer.\n\nMaybe use OpenMP parallel for over i with no blocking is simpler and might be faster due to less overhead. Let's estimate: if memory bandwidth not bottleneck, pure parallel should give ~0.17s. The best observed 0.1555 suggests maybe they used something else like vectorization or better cache.\n\nGiven our role A focus on memory locality, we can propose a candidate that uses OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule and also applies loop interchange to improve cache reuse? Actually the original inner loop already has good sequential access; but for each i it reads all p[j] sequentially. If we parallelize over i, multiple threads read same p array concurrently, which may cause memory contention but still okay.\n\nMaybe we can use OpenMP's schedule(static) with chunk size 1 to distribute i's round-robin? That would interleave i's across threads, causing each thread to access different parts of p at any time, potentially reducing cache conflicts. But static contiguous chunks are better for locality per thread (each thread processes a range of i's and reads entire p array repeatedly; but that means all threads read the same p array in order, which may cause false sharing? No, they only read).\n\nLet's look at previous role A history: experiment 10af5e5f3ddc484d got score 0.806 (mean 0.1616) and is listed as valid; that might be from another role? Actually own_role_history includes it with status valid and mean 0.1616, so it was our role A earlier! Wait the history list: \n- f2a4207ec8704f6d score 0.751\n- 545a1fb8e9284bf4 score 0.750\n- fbabdfdc3bd9402f score 0.750\n- 87e02ace96d7435f score 0.750\n- 5f6ac2dcead441ce build_failed\n- 9a9c266ca15a4f13 score 0.752 (mean 0.221)\n- 21c512f6095040bb score 0.750\n- 10af5e5f3ddc484d score 0.806 (mean 0.1616) - this is our role A! \n- 704fffe5d2a849b5 score 0.791 (mean 0.177)\n- dadbc3c742964402 score 0.750\n\nSo we already had a successful experiment with mean 0.1616 and another 0.177. The best among our role A is 10af5e5f3ddc484d at 0.806 score, mean 0.1616s. That's close to the overall best observed 0.1555 (from interaction). So we can try to improve upon that.\n\nWe don't know what source 10af5e5f3ddc484d was, but it likely used some combination of OpenMP and maybe blocking? Since its hypothesis not given in history, only outcome. But from interactions, there is an experiment with sources [\"10af5e5f3ddc484d\", \"24cc45682d584649\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] that got mean 0.1616 (same as our own? Actually interaction shows that combination gave 0.161604, which matches our own experiment 10af5e5f3ddc484d's outcome? Wait the interaction list includes an outcome with sources [\"10af5e5f3ddc484d\", \"24cc45682d584649\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] mean 0.1616, and another with [\"704fffe5d2a849b5\", \"e430f80588d44b85\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] mean 0.1555. So our own experiment 10af5e5f3ddc484d is one of the sources in that combination, but it alone might have been different? Actually the interaction outcome for [\"10af5e5f3ddc484d\", \"24cc45682d584649\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] has mean 0.1616, which matches our own experiment's mean exactly (0.161566). So that combination is likely our own experiment plus two other sources? But integration_source_ids empty means we cannot reuse them.\n\nNevertheless, we know that a source with ID 10af5e5f3ddc484d achieved 0.806 score. We need to propose a new candidate that might beat it. Since we don't have its content, we can try to infer from role A focus: memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. Likely it used SoA + OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule? Or maybe cache blocking.\n\nGiven the best observed 0.1555 uses sources including \"704fffe5d2a849b5\" (which is also in our history with score 0.791, mean 0.177) and \"e430f80588d44b85\" (unknown). So combining those two plus another gave better.\n\nWe can propose a candidate that combines:\n- Structure-of-arrays layout for positions/velocities.\n- OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule.\n- Maybe also use #pragma omp simd on inner loop? But to preserve order, we could manually unroll the j-loop by 4 and accumulate sequentially; this might allow compiler to pipeline loads.\n\nAlternatively, use cache blocking as described but with a larger tile size (e.g., 128) and SoA arrays for positions only. Let's design carefully.\n\nWe need to ensure exact output bytes. The input is binary: first int nbodies, then array of Body structs (6 floats each). We must read exactly that format. If we convert to SoA, we can allocate separate arrays and copy from the AoS data after reading. But note that the original code reads into a single malloc'd Body*; if we change layout, we need to store positions/velocities in separate arrays for computation, but at output we must write back in same AoS format (struct with x,y,z,vx,vy,vz). So we can either keep AoS and just use OpenMP, or convert to SoA internally.\n\nConverting to SoA requires copying data from input structs into separate arrays. That adds O(N) overhead once per run, negligible compared to O(N^2*10). But it also changes memory layout for computation; after simulation, we need to write back to AoS for output. We can either keep both representations or convert at end.\n\nSimpler: Keep AoS and just add OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule. That might already give ~0.17s based on our previous 10af5e5f3ddc484d? Actually that experiment got 0.1616, so maybe it used something else. Let's try to propose a candidate that uses OpenMP + cache blocking with tile size tuned.\n\nGiven the instructions: \"You propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment.\" We need to output JSON with files (src/nbody.c content), hypothesis, transformations, sources_kept empty, notes.\n\nWe should choose a transformation likely to improve over previous role A attempts. Since our best was 0.1616, and overall best is 0.1555, we can try to combine OpenMP parallelization with cache blocking using SoA layout and maybe vectorization hints that preserve order? But vectorization may not be safe.\n\nLet's think about the performance bottleneck: For N bodies, each iteration does ~N^2 force computations. With N=10000 (maybe), 1e8 ops per iter *10 = 1e9 ops. On a modern CPU with 4 cores and maybe AVX? But architecture is ARM64, so NEON SIMD available. If we can vectorize the inner loop over j using NEON while preserving exact summation order? That's hard because reduction across lanes changes order.\n\nHowever we could use pairwise summation? No, must match reference exactly.\n\nMaybe the best observed 0.1555 uses OpenMP with dynamic scheduling and maybe no blocking but also compiler auto-vectorization of the inner loop over j for each i? But if compiler vectorizes, it would change rounding; unless the reference itself was compiled with same flags and also vectorized? The baseline sequential code is given as source; when judge compiles our modified code, they compare output to official reference which likely runs the original sequential code compiled with -O3 on their machine. If both use same compiler and flags, auto-vectorization might produce identical results if the reduction order is preserved by vectorizer? Actually GCC/Clang can vectorize reductions using a fixed tree reduction that may differ from scalar order; but they often preserve exact order for floating-point unless -ffast-math is used. With default -O3 (no fast-math), compilers usually do not reorder floating-point operations due to strict IEEE compliance, so auto-vectorization of the inner loop might be disabled because it would change results. So likely no vectorization.\n\nThus performance gains come from parallelization and cache blocking.\n\nLet's design a candidate with:\n- OpenMP parallel for over i-loop (static schedule).\n- Use AoS layout (no conversion overhead).\n- Maybe add #pragma omp simd? But not safe.\n- Possibly use loop unrolling manually to improve ILP without changing order: e.g., process 4 j's per iteration but still sequential additions. That could help if compiler doesn't already do it.\n\nBut we need a novel idea relative to previous attempts. Since role A is memory locality, let's focus on cache blocking with a well-chosen tile size and maybe use SoA for positions only (x,y,z) to reduce cache footprint per body from 24 bytes to 12 bytes when reading p[j]? Actually in force computation we need x,y,z of each j; if we store separate arrays px,py,pz, then reading a contiguous block of px values is more cache-friendly. Also for tile i's, we only need their positions (x,y,z) repeatedly across j; storing them separately also helps.\n\nLet's implement SoA:\n- After reading nbodies and the AoS array into temporary buffer? Actually read_dataset currently reads directly into Body* allocated as one block. We can keep that but then create separate arrays px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz by copying from bodies. But we could also change read to allocate separate arrays and fill them; however output must be in AoS format, so at end we need to write back.\n\nSimpler: Keep the original Body* for I/O, but inside body_force use local pointers to SoA? That would require converting each iteration? No, conversion once before simulation loop. We can allocate arrays of size n for x,y,z,vx,vy,vz and copy from bodies at start; then run simulation using these arrays; after all iterations, copy back to bodies for output.\n\nBut that adds O(N) copies per run (once), negligible.\n\nAlternatively, we could change the data structure entirely: define separate arrays globally? But read_dataset returns Body*; we can modify it to return a struct containing pointers. However main expects Body*. We can keep interface but internally use SoA by converting in body_force each call? That would be O(N) per iteration (10 times), still negligible compared to O(N^2). But better to convert once.\n\nGiven the constraints, we can modify read_dataset to allocate separate arrays and return a pointer to a struct that holds them; but then main must use that. However output write_dataset expects Body* array in AoS format. We could keep both: after reading into temporary AoS buffer, copy to SoA arrays, free temp? Or just keep AoS for I/O and create SoA copies.\n\nLet's design a clean implementation:\n\n- Keep read_dataset as is (reads into Body*).\n- In main, after reading, allocate separate float arrays px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz of size nbodies.\n- Copy from bodies to these arrays.\n- Run simulation loop: for each iter, call body_force with SoA pointers; then update positions using velocities in SoA (or we can do integration inside same function).\n- After all iterations, copy back from SoA to bodies array.\n- write_dataset as before.\n\nBut this changes the structure of main. We need to ensure correctness: The original code updates velocities in body_force and then positions after each iteration. If we use SoA, we must replicate exactly.\n\nAlternatively, we can keep AoS but add OpenMP parallelization only; that's simpler and likely gives good speedup. Since our previous best 0.1616 might have been just OpenMP? Let's check: experiment 10af5e5f3ddc484d score 0.806, mean 0.1616. That is close to ideal parallel speedup (0.671/4=0.168). So it likely used OpenMP over i-loop with static schedule and maybe no blocking. If we can improve upon that by adding cache blocking or better scheduling, we might get closer to 0.1555.\n\nLet's propose a candidate that uses:\n- OpenMP parallel for over i-loop with schedule(static) (or dynamic?).\n- Additionally, use loop tiling/blocking over j within each thread? Actually if we just parallelize over i, each thread processes its chunk of i's sequentially; inside each i it loops over all j. That already has good sequential memory access per thread. The only improvement from blocking would be to reuse p[j] across multiple i's within a thread by restructuring the loop nest: for each tile of i (within thread), loop over j and compute forces for all i in tile. This reduces memory traffic.\n\nLet's implement that with AoS, using a fixed TILE size chosen as maybe 64 or 128. We'll parallelize over tiles (or over chunks of i) with OpenMP. To avoid dynamic allocation per tile, we can use local arrays on stack if TILE is small enough; but VLA not allowed in C89? The code uses C99 likely. We can define a constant TILE_SIZE and allocate float fx[TILE_SIZE] etc. inside the parallel region.\n\nBut note: OpenMP parallel for over tiles means each thread processes multiple tiles sequentially. Within each tile, we loop j from 0..n-1 and inner i in tile. This is cache-friendly because p[j] read once per tile. However the number of tiles = ceil(n/TILE). For n large (e.g., 10000), TILE=64 gives ~157 tiles; with 4 threads, each gets ~39 tiles. That's fine.\n\nWe need to ensure that the order of operations for each body is preserved: as argued, j outer and i inner preserves per-i accumulation order because we process all i in tile for each j sequentially, so for a given i, contributions from j=0..n-1 are added in increasing j. Yes.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: In original code, for each i, the loop over j computes dx = p[j].x - p[i].x; then sqrd_dist etc., and adds to fx. If we restructure with j outer, we compute for each j the same values but for all i in tile. The floating-point operations per (i,j) are identical: dx computed as px_j - px_i, etc. So bitwise same.\n\nNow choose TILE_SIZE. We want it large enough to reduce memory traffic significantly but small enough that the inner loop over i doesn't cause register spills or cache thrashing for p[i] values. Since we read p[j] once per tile and reuse across TILE i's, the total reads of p array = (n/TILE)*n * sizeof(Body). For n=10000, TILE=64 -> 1.56e6 body reads vs original 1e8? Actually original reads each j for each i: n^2 = 1e8 body reads; with TILE=64, reads ~ (10000/64)*10000 = 1.5625e6, factor 64 reduction. That should significantly reduce memory traffic if memory-bound.\n\nBut previous attempts with TILE=4 and 64 got only 0.22s, which is slower than pure OpenMP? Wait our own experiment f2a4207ec8704f6d used block size 4 and SoA, got 0.2225; that's slower than baseline sequential? No baseline 0.671, so it's faster but not as fast as pure parallel maybe. The best role A was 0.1616 (likely no blocking). So why did blocking hurt? Possibly because the inner loop over i within tile introduces extra index calculations and prevents compiler optimizations like vectorization or unrolling of the j-loop? Or because with TILE=4, overhead dominates; with TILE=64 maybe still not enough to overcome added complexity.\n\nMaybe we should try a larger TILE like 256 or 512. But then local arrays fx[256] etc. would be on stack (3*256 floats = 3072 bytes) which is okay. However the inner loop over i for each j will have many iterations; compiler might not unroll well.\n\nAlternatively, we can use OpenMP parallelization over i without blocking but with a simple #pragma omp simd? Not safe.\n\nLet's look at the best observed combination: sources [\"704fffe5d2a849b5\", \"e430f80588d44b85\", \"9adc054f311d4298\"] got 0.1555. Our own source 10af5e5f3ddc484d (score 0.806) combined with others gave 0.1616, so maybe the improvement came from adding e430f80588d44b85 and 9adc054f311d4298 to our source? But we don't know.\n\nGiven that we need one candidate, I'll propose a transformation that combines:\n- OpenMP parallelization over i-loop with static schedule.\n- Structure-of-arrays layout for positions/velocities (to improve cache line utilization and allow potential auto-vectorization of loads).\n- Maybe also use #pragma omp simd on inner loop? But to preserve order, we can manually unroll by 4 and accumulate sequentially; this might help ILP without changing rounding.\n\nActually manual unrolling with sequential additions preserves exact order: fx += term0; fx += term1; ... So it's safe. Compiler may already do that at -O3, but explicit unrolling could help if compiler doesn't due to unknown trip count? The j-loop has known n (runtime), so compiler can't fully unroll.\n\nLet's design a candidate:\n\n- Convert Body array into separate arrays px, py, pz, vx, vy, vz.\n- In body_force, use OpenMP parallel for over i with schedule(static).\n- Inside each i, compute forces using scalar code but reading from SoA arrays. This is essentially same as original except memory layout.\n\nWill this be faster than AoS? Possibly because the inner loop reads px[j], py[j], pz[j] which are contiguous streams; cache prefetcher can handle three sequential streams better than one interleaved stream of 24-byte structs. Also, each thread will read all three arrays sequentially for its i's, so memory bandwidth utilization may improve.\n\nBut we must also update velocities and positions in SoA after force computation. We'll do that inside body_force or separately.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10\n#define DT 0.01f\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f\n\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\nvoid body_force(float *px, float *py, float *pz,\n                float *vx, float *vy, float *vz,\n                int n, float dt) {\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float fx = 0.0f;\n    float fy = 0.0f;\n    float fz = 0.0f;\n\n    float xi = px[i];\n    float yi = py[i];\n    float zi = pz[i];\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      float dx = px[j] - xi;\n      float dy = py[j] - yi;\n      float dz = pz[j] - zi;\n      float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n      float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n      float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n      fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n      fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n      fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n    }\n\n    vx[i] += dt*fx;\n    vy[i] += dt*fy;\n    vz[i] += dt*fz;\n  }\n}\n\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) { ... }\n  Body *bodies = malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  ...\n  return bodies;\n}\n\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  ...\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  // Convert to SoA\n  float *px = malloc(nbodies * sizeof(float));\n  ... etc for py,pz,vx,vy,vz.\n  for (int i=0; i<nbodies; ++i) {\n    px[i]=bodies[i].x; ...\n  }\n\n  for (int iter=0; iter<N_ITER; ++iter) {\n    body_force(px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz,nbodies,DT);\n    // update positions\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i=0; i<nbodies; ++i) {\n      px[i] += vx[i]*DT;\n      py[i] += vy[i]*DT;\n      pz[i] += vz[i]*DT;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Copy back to AoS\n  for (int i=0; i<nbodies; ++i) {\n    bodies[i].x = px[i]; ...\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n  free...\n}\n```\n\nThis preserves exact floating-point operations per body because the inner loop is identical except using separate arrays. The order of additions for each i remains j=0..n-1 sequential. So output should be bitwise same.\n\nWill this improve over AoS+OpenMP? Possibly due to better cache behavior: reading three contiguous float streams vs one struct stream. Also, we can add `#pragma omp simd` on inner loop? But that would change order; not safe. However the compiler might auto-vectorize loads but not reductions.\n\nWe could also try manual unrolling of j-loop by 4 to improve ILP while preserving order:\n\n```c\nfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n   ...\n}\n```\nNo need.\n\nGiven that our previous best was likely OpenMP with AoS, this SoA version might be slightly faster. But we don't know if it will beat 0.1555.\n\nAlternatively, combine SoA with cache blocking as described earlier. Let's try to implement a blocked version with SoA and OpenMP over tiles. That would reduce memory traffic further. We can choose TILE_SIZE = 128 maybe.\n\nLet's write that:\n\n```c\n#define TILE_SIZE 64\n\nvoid body_force(float *px, float *py, float *pz,\n                float *vx, float *vy, float *vz,\n                int n, float dt) {\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int tile_start = 0; tile_start < n; tile_start += TILE_SIZE) {\n    int i_end = tile_start + TILE_SIZE;\n    if (i_end > n) i_end = n;\n    int tlen = i_end - tile_start;\n\n    float fx[TILE_SIZE], fy[TILE_SIZE], fz[TILE_SIZE];\n    for (int k=0; k<tlen; ++k) { fx[k]=fy[k]=fz[k]=0.0f; }\n\n    // Preload positions of tile bodies? Not necessary, but we can read them each j.\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      float px_j = px[j];\n      float py_j = py[j];\n      float pz_j = pz[j];\n      for (int k=0; k<tlen; ++k) {\n        int i = tile_start + k;\n        float dx = px_j - px[i];\n        float dy = py_j - py[i];\n        float dz = pz_j - pz[i];\n        float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n        float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n        float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n        fx[k] += dx * inv_dist3;\n        fy[k] += dy * inv_dist3;\n        fz[k] += dz * inv_dist3;\n      }\n    }\n\n    for (int k=0; k<tlen; ++k) {\n      int i = tile_start + k;\n      vx[i] += dt*fx[k];\n      vy[i] += dt*fy[k];\n      vz[i] += dt*fz[k];\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis uses VLA `float fx[TILE_SIZE]` inside parallel region; TILE_SIZE is constant, so it's fine. But note that each thread will have its own stack for these arrays because they are declared inside the loop body (which is executed by each thread). However OpenMP parallel for distributes iterations of tile_start across threads; each iteration has its own local fx array on stack. That's okay.\n\nBut there's a potential issue: The inner loop over k reads px[i] etc. repeatedly for each j; those are the same values across all j, so they should stay in cache if TILE_SIZE small enough (positions of tile bodies). With SoA, reading px[i], py[i], pz[i] for i in tile involves three separate arrays but contiguous within tile; that's fine.\n\nThis blocked version reduces memory reads of p[j] by factor TILE_SIZE. For n=10000 and TILE=64, total body reads ~1.56e6 vs 1e8, huge reduction. This should be much faster if memory-bound. But previous attempts with AoS+blocking got only 0.22s; maybe because they used SoA? Actually f2a4207ec8704f6d used \"structure-of-arrays layout\" and block size 4, so it was SoA+blocking but small tile. It got 0.2225. That's slower than our pure OpenMP AoS (if that gave 0.1616). Why would blocking with TILE=4 be slow? Because overhead of nested loops and small tile doesn't amortize; also maybe the compiler couldn't optimize due to variable-length arrays? But TILE_SIZE constant.\n\nMaybe with larger TILE like 128 or 256, it could be faster. Let's try TILE_SIZE = 64 first (as previous attempt used 4 and 545a1fb8e9284bf4 used 64 but got 0.2233). So TILE=64 didn't help either.\n\nWhy? Perhaps because the inner loop over k for each j has a dependency on fx[k] which prevents vectorization, and also the index calculations add overhead. Also, reading px[i]